## Purpose

This document contains only the permanent, actionable engineering rules Cursor and the Architect must check before making or approving an implementation decision.

State the rule, not the reasoning. Reasoning and long-term philosophy belong in VISION_05P.md — if you find yourself explaining *why*, that sentence belongs there, not here.

Do not add project status, temporary implementation details, current numeric defaults, or historical decisions here.

Target: 01P (~1 page)

When approaching the target size:
- Consolidate existing rules.
- Remove obsolete or superseded rules.
- If uncertain whether something belongs here, ask before adding it.

---

## Rule - Evidence Before Conclusion

Every conclusion, recommendation, or implementation decision must be supported by repository inspection, execution results, or other verifiable evidence.

Do not infer project state from previous conversations, documentation, or assumptions when current repository evidence is available.

---

## Rule - Verification Scope

Always distinguish between:

- Technical Verification (objectively verified by repository inspection, automated tests, execution, or runtime evidence)
- Gameplay / UX Evaluation (subjective observations requiring human or explicit usability review)

Automated verification must never imply gameplay quality, usability, balance, or player enjoyment unless those aspects were explicitly evaluated.


---
## Rule - Board Fidelity

Model the board using its real intersections and legal connections. Never substitute an arbitrary square grid, even where it would be simpler to code.

---

## Rule - Capture Optionality

Capturing is optional. During a capture sequence, a player — human or AI — may continue with any legal consecutive capture, or voluntarily end the sequence after completing a legal jump. This applies to every board variant and every player type.

A specific AI's decision policy for continue-vs-stop (e.g. a random opponent's internal probability) is an implementation detail of that AI only. It must never be promoted to a gameplay rule here.

---

## Rule - Configurable Parameters

Match timers, maximum plies, AI difficulty, and tournament settings must be configuration values. Gameplay logic must never hardcode them, and changing a value must never require changing gameplay logic.

Current numeric defaults (e.g. today's ply limit for Board4) are status, not rule — they belong in GPT_PROJECT_STATUS_01P.md, not here.

---

## Rule - Verified Source

The latest verified repository files are the single source of truth. Verify current state before changing anything, and verify the result immediately after. Never regenerate documentation or claims from memory or a prior conversation.

---

## Rule - Reuse Before Build

Reuse and extend existing engine components — board layouts, movement graphs, jump paths, AI logic — across variants. Do not introduce a new abstraction, file, or class unless repository inspection shows a demonstrated need.

---

## Rule - Verification Commands

Group related verification into a single command block instead of issuing multiple fragmented commands.

---

## Rule - Project Documentation Set

The short-rule and reasoning layers consist of exactly these three files:

- GPT_PROJECT_RULES_01P.md (this file — short, always loaded by Cursor)
- VISION_05P.md (long — all reasoning, philosophy, and project-specific decisions that don't fit the short-rule format)
- GPT_PROJECT_STATUS_01P.md (current phase, milestones, numeric defaults)

Agent behaviour (roles, approval tiers, workflow, safety, verification) lives in AGENT_RULE_05P.md, not here.
